#import "common.typ": *

#chapter("Chapter 3. Euclidean Vector Spaces")

#sec("3.1", "Euclidean Vector Spaces")

#subsec("Linear Operations on Vectors") // Tyrtyshnikov §9.5


Sum of vectors: let $arrow(A B) in bf(a)$ and $arrow(B C) in bf(b)$; then $bf(c) = bf(a) + bf(b)$ is defined as the vector generated by the directed segment $arrow(A C)$.

It is important that the vector $bf(c)$ obtained in this way does not depend on the choice of the point $A$. Indeed, let $arrow(P Q) in bf(a)$ and $arrow(Q R) in bf(b)$.
Then from the congruence of the triangles $triangle A B C$ and $triangle P Q R$ there follows the equality of lengths and codirectionality of the directed segments $arrow(A C)$ and $arrow(P R)$, and hence their equality.

The set of free vectors forms an abelian group with respect to the operation of vector addition. The role of the identity element is played by the zero vector $bf(0) = arrow(A A)$. For $bf(a) = arrow(A B)$, the inverse element is $bf(b) = arrow(B A)$. In this context, the vector $bf(b)$ is called the _opposite vector_ for $bf(a)$ and is denoted by $bf(b) = -bf(a)$.

Multiplication of a vector by a scalar: let $arrow(A B) in bf(a)$; then $alpha bf(a)$ is defined as the vector generated by the directed segment $arrow(A C)$ that has length $|A C| = |alpha| |A B|$ and, if $alpha != 0$, is codirectional with $arrow(A B)$ for $alpha > 0$ and oppositely directed for $alpha < 0$. It is not difficult to verify that the vector $alpha bf(a)$ does not depend on the choice of the point $A$.

One can check that $alpha(beta bf(a)) = (alpha beta) bf(a)$ for any real numbers $alpha, beta$. It is also useful to note that $1 bf(a) = bf(a)$, and the vector $(-1) bf(a)$ is the opposite of $bf(a)$.

Furthermore, the operations of vector addition and multiplication of a vector by a scalar are linked by the following distributive laws:
$ (alpha + beta) bf(a) = (alpha bf(a)) + (beta bf(a)), quad alpha(bf(a) + bf(b)) = (alpha bf(a)) + (alpha bf(b)). $

#subsec("Abstract Vector Spaces") // Tyrtyshnikov §11.1


When studying the linear dependence of vectors, linear spans, bases, and dimensions (Sections 2.3 and 3.5–3.7), we assume that vectors are column vectors with real entries. However, when studying the rank of a matrix (Section 4.2), we shall also speak of the linear dependence and independence of the rows of a matrix. Of course, from a formal standpoint, rows can be transposed so that we once again deal with column vectors. Yet all the concepts listed above and many of the facts obtained can be applied without any changes to the case where vectors are understood to be matrices of any fixed size. This fact alone prompts us to consider introducing a more general (and more abstract) concept of a vector.

Moreover, when studying bases and dimensions, we deal exclusively with linear spans of vectors, and this is not always convenient: for example, the set of all solutions of a homogeneous linear system $bf(A) bf(x) = bf(0)$ is, of course, the linear span of the vectors of a basis for the solution space, but it would be useful to have the right to study the properties of this set without mentioning the system of vectors that generates it.

Let us say that vectors are elements of some nonempty set $V$ on which two operations are defined: addition of vectors (if $bf(a), bf(b) in V$, then $bf(a) + bf(b) in V$) and multiplication of vectors by real numbers (if $bf(a) in V$ and $alpha in bb(R)$, then $alpha bf(a) in V$). Let us require that these operations possess the following properties:
- $(bf(a) + bf(b)) + bf(c) = bf(a) + (bf(b) + bf(c)) quad forall bf(a), bf(b), bf(c) in V$ (associativity of vector addition);
- there exists a special vector $bf(0)$, called the _zero vector_, such that
  $ bf(a) + bf(0) = bf(0) + bf(a) = bf(a) quad forall bf(a) in V; $
- for any vector $bf(a) in V$ there exists a vector $bf(b) in V$ such that
  $ bf(a) + bf(b) = bf(b) + bf(a) = bf(0); $
- $bf(a) + bf(b) = bf(b) + bf(a) quad forall bf(a), bf(b) in V$ (commutativity of vector addition);
- $alpha (beta bf(a)) = (alpha beta) bf(a) quad forall alpha, beta in bb(R), forall bf(a) in V$;
- $(alpha + beta) bf(a) = alpha bf(a) + beta bf(a) quad forall alpha, beta in bb(R), forall bf(a) in V$ (distributivity);
- $alpha (bf(a) + bf(b)) = alpha bf(a) + alpha bf(b) quad forall alpha in bb(R), forall bf(a), bf(b) in V$ (distributivity);
- $1 bf(a) = bf(a) quad forall bf(a) in V$.#footnote[This property is equivalent to saying that every vector $bf(a)$ can be represented in the form $bf(a) = alpha bf(b)$ for some vector $bf(b)$ and some number $alpha$. Indeed, if this property holds, one can take $bf(b) = bf(a)$ and $alpha = 1$. On the other hand, suppose this property is not assumed to hold, but it is known that $bf(a) = alpha bf(b)$. Then, using the axiom $alpha (beta bf(a)) = (alpha beta) bf(a)$, we obtain $1 (alpha bf(b)) = (1 dot alpha) bf(b) = alpha bf(b) => 1 bf(a) = bf(a)$.]

#indent A set $V$ possessing all these properties is called a (real) _vector space_, or a vector space over $bb(R)$. A frequently encountered synonymous term is _linear space_.

Note that the set $V$ with respect to the operation of vector addition is an abelian group. The role of the identity element is played by the zero vector. A vector $bf(b)$ such that $bf(a) + bf(b) = bf(b) + bf(a) = bf(0)$ is called the _opposite_ (or _negative_) of the vector $bf(a)$ and is denoted by $bf(b) = -bf(a)$.

Certain familiar properties of the operations we have introduced, which were previously applied freely to column vectors, _require proofs_ in the more abstract setting considered here.

#proposition(title: "1")[
  $0 bf(a) = bf(0) quad forall bf(a) in V$.
]

#proof[
  By distributivity,
  $ 0 bf(a) = (0 + 0) bf(a) = (0 bf(a)) + (0 bf(a)). $
  Further, let $bf(b) = -(0 bf(a))$ (the vector opposite to the vector $0 bf(a)$). Then $bf(0) = bf(b) + (0 bf(a)) = (bf(b) + (0 bf(a))) + (0 bf(a)) => bf(0) = 0 bf(a)$.
]

#proposition(title: "2")[
  $alpha bf(0) = bf(0) quad forall alpha in bb(R)$.
]

#proof[
  $alpha bf(0) = alpha (bf(0) + bf(0)) = alpha bf(0) + alpha bf(0) => alpha bf(0) = bf(0)$.
]

#proposition(title: "3")[
  $(-1) bf(a) = -bf(a) quad forall bf(a) in V$.
]

#proof[
  By Proposition 1 and distributivity,
  $ bf(0) = 0 bf(a) = (1 + (-1)) bf(a) = (1 bf(a)) + ((-1) bf(a)) = bf(a) + ((-1) bf(a)). $
]

#proposition(title: "4")[
  If $alpha bf(a) = bf(0)$, then either $alpha = 0$ or $bf(a) = bf(0)$.
]

#proof[
  Suppose#footnote[This proposition cannot be obtained without the axiom $1 bf(a) = bf(a)$. Indeed, take any abelian group $V$ with zero element $bf(0)$ and define multiplication by a number by the rule $alpha bf(a) = bf(0)$ for all numbers $alpha$ and vectors $bf(a) in V$. Then all the axioms of a vector space will be satisfied except this one.] $alpha != 0$. Then
  $ bf(a) = 1 bf(a) = (1/alpha alpha) bf(a) = 1/alpha (alpha bf(a)) = 1/alpha bf(0) = bf(0). $
]

#indent As before, for any numbers $alpha_1, ..., alpha_n$, a vector $bf(w)$ of the form
$ bf(w) = alpha_1 bf(a)_1 + ... + alpha_n bf(a)_n $
is called a linear combination of the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n$, and the set of all linear combinations with all possible values of the coefficients $alpha_1, ..., alpha_n$ is called the linear span of the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n$ and is denoted by $span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n}$.

Note that the zero vector is denoted by $bf(0)$, to distinguish it from the number $0$.

#sec("3.2", "Dot Product, Norm, Distance")

#subsec("Dot Product of Geometric Vectors") // Tyrtyshnikov §10.1


The length of a vector $bf(a)$ is defined as the length of a directed segment generating it (directed segments generating one and the same vector are equal and therefore have the same length). Notation for the length: $norm(bf(a))$. The angle $theta$ between nonzero vectors $bf(a) = arrow(O A)$, $bf(b) = arrow(O B)$ is defined as the angle between the sides $O A$ and $O B$ in the triangle $O A B$.

The _dot product_ (or _scalar product_) of vectors $bf(a)$ and $bf(b)$ is the quantity
$ bf(a) dot bf(b) = cases(
  norm(bf(a)) norm(bf(b)) cos theta\, & quad bf(a) != bf(0) "and" bf(b) != bf(0)\,,
  0\, & quad bf(a) = bf(0) "or" bf(b) = bf(0).
) $
By virtue of the definition, it is obvious that
#eq(
  $ bf(a) dot bf(a) > 0 quad "for " bf(a) != bf(0); quad bf(a) dot bf(a) = 0 <=> bf(a) = bf(0). $,
  $(1)$
)
It is also obvious that
#eq(
  $ bf(a) dot bf(b) = bf(b) dot bf(a) quad forall bf(a), bf(b). $,
  $(2)$
)

#indent If the vectors $bf(a) = arrow(O A)$, $bf(b) = arrow(O B)$ are noncollinear, then in the plane passing through the points $O, A, B$ one can introduce a Cartesian coordinate system with origin at the point $O$ and first axis coinciding with the line $O B$ and giving the point $B$ a positive coordinate. Then the quantity $norm(bf(a)) cos theta$ will be precisely the coordinate of the point $A$ on this axis. From this there immediately follow the important properties of linearity of the dot product in the first argument:
#eq(
  $ (bf(a) + bf(b)) dot bf(c) = bf(a) dot bf(c) + bf(b) dot bf(c) quad forall bf(a), bf(b), bf(c), $,
  $(3)$
)
#eq(
  $ (alpha bf(a)) dot bf(b) = alpha (bf(a) dot bf(b)) quad forall bf(a), bf(b), quad forall alpha in bb(R). $,
  $(4)$
)
Property $(2)$ immediately yields the analogous properties of linearity of the dot product in the second argument as well.

Vectors are called _orthogonal_ if their dot product is equal to zero.

#subsec("Dot Product and Coordinates") // Tyrtyshnikov §10.2


Suppose a Cartesian coordinate system with basis vectors $bf(e)_1, bf(e)_2, bf(e)_3$ is given. Then
#eq(
  $ bf(e)_i dot bf(e)_j = cases(
    0\, & quad i != j\,,
    1\, & quad i = j.
  ) $,
  $(5)$
)

#theorem[
  Suppose that in a given Cartesian coordinate system the vector $bf(a)$ has coordinates $a_1, a_2, a_3$, and the vector $bf(b)$ has coordinates $b_1, b_2, b_3$. Then the formula
  #eq(
    $ bf(a) dot bf(b) = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + a_3 b_3 $,
    $(6)$
  )
  holds.
]

#proof[
  Let us write
  $ bf(a) = a_1 bf(e)_1 + a_2 bf(e)_2 + a_3 bf(e)_3, quad bf(b) = b_1 bf(e)_1 + b_2 bf(e)_2 + b_3 bf(e)_3. $
  Relying on properties $(2)$–$(4)$ of the dot product and relations $(5)$, we find
  $ bf(a) dot bf(b) &= (a_1 bf(e)_1 + a_2 bf(e)_2 + a_3 bf(e)_3) dot (b_1 bf(e)_1 + b_2 bf(e)_2 + b_3 bf(e)_3) = \
  &= sum_(i=1)^3 sum_(j=1)^3 a_i b_j (bf(e)_i dot bf(e)_j) =a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + a_3 b_3. $
]

#remark(title: "1")[
  If in some coordinate system the dot product of any vectors $bf(a)$ and $bf(b)$ is computed by formula $(6)$, then that coordinate system is Cartesian.
]

#remark(title: "2")[
  In the case of a Cartesian coordinate system for vectors in a plane, formula $(6)$ takes the form
  $ bf(a) dot bf(b) = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2. $
]

#subsec("On Generalizations") // Tyrtyshnikov §10.3


Formula $(6)$ and properties $(1)$–$(4)$ provide a basis for introducing an inner product in more general cases—for objects that are no longer vectors in geometric space.
For example, if $bf(a) = (a_1, ..., a_n)^T$, $bf(b) = (b_1, ..., b_n)^T$ are column vectors in $bb(R)^n$, one can define their dot product by analogy with formula $(6)$:
#eq(
  $ bf(a) dot bf(b) = a_1 b_1 + ... + a_n b_n. $,
  $(7)$
)
There is also another idea of a more general character—to take properties $(1)$–$(4)$ as the foundation and call any function of column vectors $bf(a), bf(b)$ satisfying axioms $(1)$–$(4)$ an inner product.

For geometric vectors, the dot product was defined on the basis of concepts such as the length of a vector and the angle between vectors. In more general cases, it is simpler to introduce an inner product in some way and then use it to introduce the concepts of length (norm) and angle:
$ norm(bf(a)) = sqrt(bf(a) dot bf(a)), quad cos theta = (bf(a) dot bf(b)) / (norm(bf(a)) norm(bf(b))). $

#indent For example, relying on $(7)$, one can introduce length and angle in this way for vectors $bf(a), bf(b) in bb(R)^n$. Here it is important that
$ |bf(a) dot bf(b)| <= sqrt(bf(a) dot bf(a)) sqrt(bf(b) dot bf(b)). $
This inequality (known as the Cauchy–Bunyakovsky–Schwarz inequality) is easily deduced from $(7)$, but in fact it holds for all conceivable ways of specifying an inner product—we shall discuss this in detail later in this section.

#subsec("Euclidean Space") // Tyrtyshnikov §24.1


Let $V$ be a real vector space on which to each ordered pair of vectors $bf(x), bf(y) in V$ there is assigned a real number $ip(bf(x), bf(y))$ in such a way that:
#[
  #set enum(numbering: "(i)")
  + $ip(bf(x), bf(x)) >= 0 quad forall bf(x) in V; quad ip(bf(x), bf(x)) = 0 <=> bf(x) = bf(0)$;
  + $ip(bf(x), bf(y)) = ip(bf(y), bf(x)) quad forall bf(x), bf(y) in V$;
  + $ip(bf(x) + bf(y), bf(z)) = ip(bf(x), bf(z)) + ip(bf(y), bf(z)) quad forall bf(x), bf(y), bf(z) in V$;
  + $ip(alpha bf(x), bf(y)) = alpha ip(bf(x), bf(y)) quad forall alpha in bb(R), quad forall bf(x) in V$.
]

#indent The number $ip(bf(x), bf(y))$ is called the _inner product_ (or _scalar product_) of the vectors $bf(x)$ and $bf(y)$. A real vector space with an inner product is called _Euclidean_.

In $bb(R)^n$, the inner product of vectors $bf(x) = (x_1, ..., x_n)^T$ and $bf(y) = (y_1, ..., y_n)^T$ is often introduced as the sum of pairwise products of their coordinates:
#eq($ bf(x) dot bf(y) = sum_(i=1)^n x_i y_i = bf(y)^T bf(x). $, $(8)$)
It is called the _natural_ inner product on $bb(R)^n$; this is the dot product.

However, on $bb(R)^n$ an inner product can also be introduced in many other ways: for example, if we fix numbers $lambda_1, ..., lambda_n > 0$, then the expression
$ ip(bf(x), bf(y)) = sum_(i=1)^n lambda_i x_i y_i = bf(y)^T mat(lambda_1, , 0; , dots.down, ; 0, , lambda_n) bf(x) $
possesses properties (i)–(iv) and, consequently, defines an inner product.

#subsec("Length of a Vector", beyond: true) // Tyrtyshnikov §24.4


Let $V$ be an arbitrary space with an inner product. The quantity
$ norm(bf(x)) = sqrt(ip(bf(x), bf(x))) $
is called the _length_ of the vector $bf(x) in V$.

#theorem(title: "Cauchy–Bunyakovsky–Schwarz Inequality")[
  For any vectors $bf(x), bf(y) in V$,
  #eq($ abs(ip(bf(x), bf(y))) <= norm(bf(x)) norm(bf(y)), $, $(9)$)
  and equality is attained if and only if $bf(x)$ and $bf(y)$ are linearly dependent.
]

#proof[
  Let us write the complex number $ip(bf(x), bf(y))$ in trigonometric form:
  $ ip(bf(x), bf(y)) = abs(ip(bf(x), bf(y))) xi, quad xi = cos phi + i sin phi. $
  If $bf(y) = bf(0)$, then equality holds in $(9)$. Suppose $bf(y) != bf(0)$. For an arbitrary $t in bb(R)$, consider the expression
  $ ip(bf(x) + t xi bf(y), bf(x) + t xi bf(y)) = ip(bf(x), bf(x)) + t overline(xi) ip(bf(x), bf(y)) + t xi overline(ip(bf(x), bf(y))) + t^2 xi overline(xi) ip(bf(y), bf(y)) = \
  = t^2 norm(bf(y))^2 + 2 t abs(ip(bf(x), bf(y))) + norm(bf(x))^2 >= 0. $
  The nonnegativity of the quadratic trinomial in the variable $t$ means that its discriminant is nonpositive:
  $ D = abs(ip(bf(x), bf(y)))^2 - norm(bf(x))^2 norm(bf(y))^2 <= 0 quad => quad abs(ip(bf(x), bf(y))) <= norm(bf(x)) norm(bf(y)). $
  Suppose that for $bf(y) != bf(0)$ equality holds in $(9)$ $=>$ $D = 0 =>$ for some real $t$ we obtain $ip(bf(x) + t xi bf(y), bf(x) + t xi bf(y)) = 0 => bf(x) + t xi bf(y) = bf(0)$. It is also obvious that if $bf(y) = bf(0)$ or $bf(x) = alpha bf(y)$, then $(9)$ turns into an equality.
]

#corollary[
  Length is a vector norm on $V$.
]

#proof[
  The first two properties of a norm are obvious, and the triangle inequality follows from the Cauchy–Bunyakovsky–Schwarz inequality:
  $ norm(bf(x) + bf(y))^2 = norm(bf(x))^2 + norm(bf(y))^2 + ip(bf(x), bf(y)) + ip(bf(y), bf(x)) <= \
  <= norm(bf(x))^2 + norm(bf(y))^2 + 2 norm(bf(x)) norm(bf(y)) = (norm(bf(x)) + norm(bf(y)))^2. $
]

#indent A space with an inner product that is complete with respect to the norm $norm(dot)$ is usually called a _Hilbert_ space.

#problem(number: 1)[
