#sec("3.4", "Subspaces")

#subsec("Subspaces of a Vector Space") // Tyrtyshnikov §11.5


A nonempty set $W subset.eq V$ is called a _subspace_ of the vector space $V$ if it is itself a vector space with respect to the operations acting in $V$. Clearly, in order for $W$ to be a subspace, it is necessary and sufficient that for any vectors $bf(a), bf(b) in W$ and any number $alpha$ the inclusions $bf(a) + bf(b) in W$ and $alpha bf(a) in W$ hold.

If the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n$ belong to a subspace $W$, then
$ span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n} subset.eq W. $

#example[
  Consider the set $V$ of all free vectors in the plane with a coordinate system centered at the point $O$. Since each free vector is generated by one and only one position vector, any subset of free vectors can be identified with a subset of position vectors $arrow(O A)$ or their endpoints—the points $A$.

  The set $V$ is obviously a vector space. Any line passing through the origin is a subspace in $V$. At the same time, if $l$ is a line not passing through the origin, then it is not a subspace: let $A, B in l$ and $arrow(O C) = arrow(O A) + arrow(O B)$; clearly, $C notin l$.
]

#problem(number: 1)[
  Prove that the vector space $bb(R)^n$ cannot be represented as the union of a finite number of sets, each of which does not coincide with $bb(R)^n$ and is a subspace of it.
]

#subsec("Sum and Intersection of Subspaces", beyond: true) // Tyrtyshnikov §11.6


Let $P$ and $Q$ be subspaces of a vector space $V$. By the sum $P + Q$ we mean the set of all vectors of the form $bf(w) = bf(p) + bf(q)$, where $bf(p) in P$, $bf(q) in Q$. By the intersection $P sect Q$ we mean the usual intersection of sets.

#proposition[
  The sets $P + Q$ and $P sect Q$ are subspaces in $V$.
]

#proof[
  1) Consider an arbitrary linear combination of vectors $bf(w)_1, bf(w)_2 in P + Q$. By the definition of the set $P + Q$ we have: $bf(w)_1 = bf(p)_1 + bf(q)_1$ and $bf(w)_2 = bf(p)_2 + bf(q)_2$, where $bf(p)_1, bf(p)_2 in P$ and $bf(q)_1, bf(q)_2 in Q$. Then
  $ alpha_1 bf(w)_1 + alpha_2 bf(w)_2 = (alpha_1 bf(p)_1 + alpha_2 bf(p)_2) + (alpha_1 bf(q)_1 + alpha_2 bf(q)_2) in P + Q, $
  since the vector in the first parentheses belongs to $P$ and the vector in the second parentheses belongs to $Q$ ($P$ and $Q$ are subspaces, and therefore they contain all linear combinations of their vectors).

  2) Similarly, consider a linear combination of vectors $bf(w)_1, bf(w)_2 in P sect Q$:
  $ alpha_1 bf(w)_1 + alpha_2 bf(w)_2 in P quad "and simultaneously" quad alpha_1 bf(w)_1 + alpha_2 bf(w)_2 in Q \
  => alpha_1 bf(w)_1 + alpha_2 bf(w)_2 in P sect Q. $
]

Note that any two subspaces have a nonempty intersection: each of them contains at least the zero vector.

#theorem(title: "Grassmann's Theorem")[
  Let $W_1$ and $W_2$ be finite-dimensional subspaces of a vector space $V$. Then
  $ dim(W_1 + W_2) = dim(W_1) + dim(W_2) - dim(W_1 sect W_2). $
]

#proof[
  Consider a basis ${bf(g)_1, ..., bf(g)_r}$ for the subspace $W_1 sect W_2$ and extend it first to a basis for $W_1$:
  $ {bf(g)_1, ..., bf(g)_r, bf(p)_1, ..., bf(p)_k}, quad r + k = dim(W_1), $
  and then to a basis for $W_2$:
  $ {bf(g)_1, ..., bf(g)_r, bf(q)_1, ..., bf(q)_m}, quad r + m = dim(W_2). $
  Obviously,
  $ W_1 + W_2 = span{bf(g)_1, ..., bf(g)_r, bf(p)_1, ..., bf(p)_k, bf(q)_1, ..., bf(q)_m}. $
  Therefore, it remains to prove the linear independence of the vectors generating this linear span. Let
  $ alpha_1 bf(g)_1 + ... + alpha_r bf(g)_r + beta_1 bf(p)_1 + ... + beta_k bf(p)_k + gamma_1 bf(q)_1 + ... + gamma_m bf(q)_m = bf(0). $
  From this,
  $ alpha_1 bf(g)_1 + ... + alpha_r bf(g)_r + beta_1 bf(p)_1 + ... + beta_k bf(p)_k = -(gamma_1 bf(q)_1 + ... + gamma_m bf(q)_m) in W_1 sect W_2. $
  Since $W_1 sect W_2 = span{bf(g)_1, ..., bf(g)_r}$, for some coefficients $delta_1, ..., delta_r$ we have
  $ delta_1 bf(g)_1 + ... + delta_r bf(g)_r = -(gamma_1 bf(q)_1 + ... + gamma_m bf(q)_m), $
  which is equivalent to the equality
  $ delta_1 bf(g)_1 + ... + delta_r bf(g)_r + gamma_1 bf(q)_1 + ... + gamma_m bf(q)_m = bf(0) => \
  => delta_1 = ... = delta_r = gamma_1 = ... = gamma_m = 0 => \
  => alpha_1 = ... = alpha_r = beta_1 = ... = beta_k = 0. $
]

