#subsec("Transitivity of Linear Dependence") // Tyrtyshnikov §3.5


An important (though obvious) property: if
$ span{bf(c)_1, ..., bf(c)_r} subset.eq span{bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} \
"and" quad span{bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} subset.eq span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}, $
then
$ span{bf(c)_1, ..., bf(c)_r} subset.eq span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}. $

#subsec("Monotonicity of the Number of Linearly Independent Vectors") // Tyrtyshnikov §3.6


#lemma(title: "4")[
  Suppose that each of the systems of vectors $bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m$ and $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$ is linearly independent, and suppose that
  #eq($ span{bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} subset.eq span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}. $, $(*)$)
  Then $m <= k$.
]

#proof[
  According to $(*)$, the system
  $ bf(b)_1, bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k $
  is linearly dependent. By Lemma 1, there exists a vector that is a linear combination of the preceding vectors; let this be the vector
  $ bf(a)_k in span{bf(b)_1, bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(k-1)}. $
  From this it follows that
  $ span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k} subset.eq span{bf(b)_1, bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(k-1)}. $
  By the transitivity of linear dependence,
  $ span{bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} subset.eq span{bf(b)_1, bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(k-1)}, $
  and therefore the system
  $ bf(b)_2, bf(b)_1, bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(k-1) $
  is linearly dependent. By Lemma 1, in this system too there exists a vector that is linearly expressible in terms of the preceding ones, and this vector cannot be $bf(b)_1$ (the vectors $bf(b)_1, bf(b)_2$ are linearly independent as a subsystem of a linearly independent system (Lemma 2)). Without loss of generality, we may assume that
  $ bf(a)_(k-1) in span{bf(b)_2, bf(b)_1, bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(k-2)}. $

  #indent Suppose that $m > k$. Then, continuing the preceding constructions, at the $k$-th step we obtain
  $ span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k} subset.eq span{bf(b)_k, bf(b)_(k-1), ..., bf(b)_1}. $
  Consequently, $bf(b)_(k+1) in span{bf(b)_k, bf(b)_(k-1), ..., bf(b)_1}$, which contradicts the assumption that the vectors $bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m$ are linearly independent. The resulting contradiction proves that $m <= k$.
]

#indent Note that the lemma remains valid even without the assumption that the system $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$ is linearly independent—prove this!

#sec("3.6", "Basis and Coordinates")

#subsec("Basis and Dimension") // Tyrtyshnikov §3.7


A linearly independent set of vectors ${bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} subset.eq V = span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}$ is called a _basis_ for the linear span $V$ if $span{bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} = V$.

#theorem(title: "Basis Theorem")[
  Any bases for a linear span $V$ contain one and the same number of vectors.
]

#proof[
  Let ${bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m}$ and ${bf(c)_1, ..., bf(c)_r}$ be two bases for the given linear span. Clearly,
  $ span{bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} = span{bf(c)_1, ..., bf(c)_r}. $
  Applying Lemma 4 (Section 3.5) twice, we obtain two inequalities: $m <= r$ and $r <= m$. Hence $m = r$.
]

#definition[
  The number of vectors in the bases for a linear span $V$ is called its _dimension_ and is denoted by $dim(V)$.
]

#theorem(title: "Dimension of a Linear Span")[
  $ dim(span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}) <= k. $
]

#proof[
  It suffices to note that as a basis for the linear span of a given system of vectors one can choose a maximal linearly independent subsystem of it.
]

#indent As a basis for the linear span $span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n}$, one can always choose some subsystem of the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n$. A maximal linearly independent subsystem is called a _base_ of the given system.

#proposition[
  For a subsystem of the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n$ to be a basis for $V = span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n}$, it is necessary and sufficient that it be a base.
]

#proof[
  Without loss of generality, we may assume that the subsystem is formed by the first $k$ vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$. If this is a base, then each of the vectors $bf(a)_(k+1), ..., bf(a)_n$ is linearly expressible in terms of $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k => V subset.eq span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k} subset.eq V => V = span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}$. Thus, ${bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}$ is a basis for $V$.

  If the chosen subsystem is a basis for $V$, then by the preceding argument and the Basis Theorem, no base can have a larger number of vectors.
]

#problem(number: 1)[
  The vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(k+1)$ are linearly independent. Prove that in the linear span $span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_(k+1)}$ there exists a basis containing no vector from the linear span $span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}$.
