#subsec("Basis and Dimension of an Abstract Vector Space", beyond: true) // Tyrtyshnikov §11.4


Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space. By definition, it is the linear span of a finite number of its vectors:
$ V = span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n}. $
The concepts of linearly dependent and linearly independent systems of vectors in the abstract case do not differ in any way from the same concepts in the case of column vectors. The same holds with regard to basis and dimension:
- $V$ can be represented as the linear span of some linearly independent subsystem of the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n$;
- a basis for the space $V$ is defined as any linearly independent set of vectors for which $V$ is the linear span; any two bases for $V$ contain the same number of vectors; the number of vectors in a basis is called the dimension of the space $V$ and is denoted by $dim(V)$;
- any linearly independent set of vectors from $V$ can be extended to a basis for $V$; moreover, this can be done using some of the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_n$.

#indent The proofs of these propositions repeat the proofs given in Sections 3.5–3.7 for the special case where vectors are understood to be column vectors.

#subsec("Expansion in a Basis") // Tyrtyshnikov §12.1


Let $V$ be a real vector space of dimension $n$ and $T = {bf(f)_1, ..., bf(f)_n}$ be some basis for it. Then any vector $bf(v) in V$ has a unique expansion in this basis:
$ bf(v) = x_1 bf(f)_1 + ... + x_n bf(f)_n. $
The coefficients $x_1, ..., x_n$ are called the coordinates of the vector $bf(v)$ relative to the basis $T$. Clearly, between the elements of the vector space $V$ and the set of columns $bb(R)^n$ there is a one-to-one correspondence:
$ bf(v) <-> [bf(v)]_T = bf(x) = mat(x_1; dots.v; x_n). $
Upon choosing another basis $S = {bf(g)_1, ..., bf(g)_n}$, there arises one more one-to-one correspondence between the same sets:
$ bf(v) = y_1 bf(g)_1 + ... + y_n bf(g)_n <-> [bf(v)]_S = bf(y) = mat(y_1; dots.v; y_n). $
Consider the expansions
#eq(
  $ bf(f)_1 &= p_(1 1) bf(g)_1 + ... + p_(n 1) bf(g)_n, \
    & dots.h.c \
    bf(f)_n &= p_(1 n) bf(g)_1 + ... + p_(n n) bf(g)_n $,
  $(*)$
)
and introduce the $n times n$ matrix $bf(P) = (p_(i j))$; its $j$th column is the coordinate vector $[bf(f)_j]_S$, that is,
$ bf(P) = mat([bf(f)_1]_S, dots.c, [bf(f)_n]_S). $
Substituting $(*)$ into the expansion of the vector $bf(v)$ in the basis $T$, we find
#eq($ [bf(v)]_S = bf(P) [bf(v)]_T, quad "i.e." quad bf(y) = bf(P) bf(x). $, $(**)$)
This relation makes it possible to pass from the coordinates of a vector relative to the basis $T$ to the coordinates of the same vector relative to the basis $S$.

By virtue of $(**)$, the matrix $bf(P)$ is called the _transition matrix from $T$ to $S$_. If $bf(f)_i$ and $bf(g)_i$ are the columns of coordinates of the corresponding vectors in some third basis, then according to $(*)$ we have
$ mat(bf(f)_1, dots.c, bf(f)_n) = mat(bf(g)_1, dots.c, bf(g)_n) bf(P), $
whence follows the invertibility of the matrix $bf(P)$ and the fact that $bf(P)^(-1)$ is the transition matrix from $S$ to $T$. Since this equality expresses the vectors $bf(f)_j$ through the vectors $bf(g)_i$, some books (the full textbook among them) use the opposite name and call $bf(P)$ the transition matrix from ${bf(g)_i}$ to ${bf(f)_i}$, that is, from $S$ to $T$. However, the name is not what matters—what is important is that the matrix $bf(P)$ be used properly when recalculating coordinates!

#sec("3.7", "Dimensions")

#subsec("Completion to a Basis") // Tyrtyshnikov §3.8


#lemma(title: "Basis Extension Lemma")[
  Every linearly independent set of vectors ${bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m} subset.eq span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}$ is a subset of some basis for the given linear span.
]

#proof[
  It suffices to consider the case where the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$ are linearly independent. The system of vectors $bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m, bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$ is linearly dependent. By Lemma 1 (Section 3.5), there exists in it a vector that is linearly expressible in terms of the preceding ones. Let us remove this vector and consider the remaining subsystem. If it is linearly independent, then it is a basis for the linear span $span{bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k}$. If not, there is a vector in it that is linearly expressible in terms of the preceding ones. We remove it from the system as well, consider the remaining subsystem, and so on. Ultimately, the system of vectors $bf(b)_1, ..., bf(b)_m$ will be completed to a basis by some of the vectors $bf(a)_1, ..., bf(a)_k$.
]

#subsec("Existence of a Basis") // Tyrtyshnikov §3.9


Does a basis exist for every linear span? By the Basis Extension Lemma, a basis exists if the linear span contains a linearly independent subsystem of vectors. This will be the case if there exists at least one nonzero vector.

Thus, there is no basis only in the case of the zero space ${bf(0)}$, the linear span which contains a single vector—the zero vector. By definition, the dimension of the zero space is equal to zero. (MA1522 regards the empty set $emptyset$ as the basis for the zero space; this agrees with $dim({bf(0)}) = 0$.)

#sec("3.8", "Transition Matrices")

#see-section("3.6", [Expansion in a Basis])

#subsec("Transformation of Coordinates") // Tyrtyshnikov §9.11


Suppose we have two affine coordinate systems: the first centered at the point $O$ with basis vectors $bf(e)_1, bf(e)_2, bf(e)_3$, and the second centered at the point $O'$ with basis vectors $bf(e)'_1, bf(e)'_2, bf(e)'_3$. Let us write
$ bf(e)'_1 &= p_(1 1) bf(e)_1 + p_(2 1) bf(e)_2 + p_(3 1) bf(e)_3, \
bf(e)'_2 &= p_(1 2) bf(e)_1 + p_(2 2) bf(e)_2 + p_(3 2) bf(e)_3, \
bf(e)'_3 &= p_(1 3) bf(e)_1 + p_(2 3) bf(e)_2 + p_(3 3) bf(e)_3 $
and form the _transition matrix_ from the basis ${bf(e)'_1, bf(e)'_2, bf(e)'_3}$ to the basis ${bf(e)_1, bf(e)_2, bf(e)_3}$ (named as in Section 3.6)
$ bf(P) = mat(
  p_(1 1), p_(1 2), p_(1 3);
  p_(2 1), p_(2 2), p_(2 3);
  p_(3 1), p_(3 2), p_(3 3)
). $
If there is some third system of basis vectors and the vectors $bf(e)_i, bf(e)'_i$ are regarded as columns of the coordinates of their expansions in this third system, then the equality
$ mat(bf(e)'_1, bf(e)'_2, bf(e)'_3) = mat(bf(e)_1, bf(e)_2, bf(e)_3) bf(P) $
holds. From this follows the invertibility of the matrix $bf(P)$.

Suppose a point $M$ has coordinates $(x, y, z)$ in the first system and $(x', y', z')$ in the second system. Obviously, $arrow(O M) = arrow(O O') + arrow(O' M)$. Let the coordinates of the point $O'$ in the first system be $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$. Then
$ x bf(e)_1 + y bf(e)_2 + z bf(e)_3 &= (x_0 bf(e)_1 + y_0 bf(e)_2 + z_0 bf(e)_3) + x'(p_(1 1) bf(e)_1 + p_(2 1) bf(e)_2 + p_(3 1) bf(e)_3) + \
&+ y'(p_(1 2) bf(e)_1 + p_(2 2) bf(e)_2 + p_(3 2) bf(e)_3) + z'(p_(1 3) bf(e)_1 + p_(2 3) bf(e)_2 + p_(3 3) bf(e)_3) = \
&= (x_0 + p_(1 1) x' + p_(1 2) y' + p_(1 3) z') bf(e)_1 + \
&+ (y_0 + p_(2 1) x' + p_(2 2) y' + p_(2 3) z') bf(e)_2 + \
&+ (z_0 + p_(3 1) x' + p_(3 2) y' + p_(3 3) z') bf(e)_3. $
Consequently, the coordinates of one and the same point in the first and second coordinate systems are related by the following formula:
$ mat(x; y; z) = mat(x_0; y_0; z_0) + bf(P) mat(x'; y'; z'). $

#indent From this it is easy to understand, for example, how the general equations of one and the same plane in different affine coordinate systems are related. If in the first coordinate system we have the equation $A x + B y + C z + D = 0$, then, writing it in matrix form, we find
$ mat(A, B, C) mat(x; y; z) = -D quad <=> quad mat(A, B, C) (mat(x_0; y_0; z_0) + bf(P) mat(x'; y'; z')) = -D. $
Thus, the equation of the same plane in the second system takes the form
$ mat(A', B', C') mat(x'; y'; z') = -D', \
mat(A', B', C') = mat(A, B, C) bf(P), quad D' = D + (A x_0 + B y_0 + C z_0). $
